"1. Use LensKit version 0.14.4 for all recommendation, splitting, and evaluation code, and ensure the environment pins compatible dependency versions.\n2. Load the three raw input files from the working directory exactly as: `u.data` (MovieLens100K), `VideoGames.csv` (Amazon Video Games), and `user_taggedartists-timestamps.dat` (Last.FM).\n3. Parse each dataset into a common implicit-feedback interaction table with at least `user` and `item` columns; preserve original IDs or map them consistently per dataset.\n4. For MovieLens100K and Amazon Video Games, convert to implicit interactions by keeping only rows with rating > 3 and dropping all other ratings.\n5. For Last.FM, treat each observed user-item event as an implicit interaction; remove duplicate user-item pairs so each interaction is binary.\n6. Apply iterative 5-core filtering separately to each dataset after implicit conversion, ensuring every remaining user and every remaining item has at least 5 interactions.\n7. Report the post-filtering dataset sizes per dataset: number of users, items, and interactions.\n8. Generate exactly 5 distinct random seeds for splitting, record them, and reuse the same seed list across all algorithms and datasets.\n9. For each dataset and seed, perform a user-based 80/20 holdout split so each user contributes approximately 20% of their interactions to test and 80% to train, with safeguards for users having very small interaction counts after 5-core filtering.\n10. Ensure train/test splits are created independently for each seed and dataset, but all algorithms on the same dataset-seed pair use the identical split.\n11. Train exactly three algorithms on each train split using standard/default LensKit 0.14.4 hyperparameters: ALS, ItemKNN, and Pop.\n12. Configure the algorithms for implicit-feedback recommendation as supported in LensKit 0.14.4; do not use rating prediction objectives.\n13. Generate top-N recommendations for each test user from each trained model, excluding items seen in that user\u2019s training history.\n14. Evaluate recommendation quality using nDCG@1, nDCG@5, nDCG@10 and Precision@1, Precision@5, Precision@10.\n15. Compute metrics against each user\u2019s held-out test interactions and aggregate results at minimum by mean over users for every dataset \u00d7 algorithm \u00d7 seed condition.\n16. Save detailed per-user metric outputs and summarized aggregate tables to disk in a reproducible format such as CSV.\n17. Produce a final summary table covering all 45 runs (3 datasets \u00d7 3 algorithms \u00d7 5 seeds) with all six metrics and the corresponding seed.\n18. Quantify seed sensitivity for each dataset-algorithm-metric combination using descriptive statistics across the 5 seeds, including mean, standard deviation, and coefficient of variation or range.\n19. Conduct a short statistical analysis across seeds, such as repeated-run variability summaries and confidence intervals or one-way ANOVA/nonparametric alternatives where appropriate, without overstating significance given only 5 seeds.\n20. Include comparisons of between-seed variability versus between-algorithm performance differences to support conclusions about how much split randomness affects accuracy.\n21. Set and log all random states used by splitting and any stochastic training procedures so runs are reproducible.\n22. Handle dataset-specific parsing issues robustly, including delimiters, headers, timestamps, and duplicate interactions, without crashing.\n23. Ensure evaluation only includes users present in both train and test with at least one relevant held-out item.\n24. Structure the code so preprocessing, splitting, training, evaluation, and analysis are modular and can be rerun independently.\n25. Print or save a concise experiment report summarizing preprocessing outcomes, metric results, and the statistical interpretation of seed effects."